DECISIONS & REASONING
---------------------

Decision 1: Use Real Backend Testing Pattern (No API Mocking)
  - Context: Dashboard aggregates data from IndexedDB via Zustand stores
  - Rationale: Playwright config includes webServer that runs dev server with real IndexedDB
  - Evidence: Existing tests (checkin.spec.ts, log-page.spec.ts) use real backend
  - Trade-offs: Tests are slightly slower but verify complete integration
  - Impact: Tests verify actual data flow from stores to UI components

Decision 2: Test Both "No Data" and "With Data" States
  - Context: Dashboard shows different content for new vs active users
  - Rationale: New users see welcome messages, active users see actual data/insights
  - Evidence: dashboardStore.ts has conditional logic based on data presence
  - Trade-offs: More tests to maintain, but critical for UX
  - Impact: Ensures proper UX for both user states

Decision 3: Create Test Data Programmatically
  - Context: Tests need data to verify "with data" state
  - Rationale: Navigate to /log and /checkin to create real entries in IndexedDB
  - Evidence: Existing checkin.spec.ts tests create multiple entries this way
  - Trade-offs: Tests depend on /log and /checkin working, but more realistic
  - Impact: Tests verify real user workflows end-to-end

Decision 4: Comprehensive Responsive Testing (3 viewports)
  - Context: PWA must work on mobile, tablet, desktop
  - Rationale: Dashboard is main landing page, must be responsive
  - Evidence: Existing pwa/responsive.spec.ts tests multiple viewports
  - Trade-offs: More test execution time, but critical for PWA
  - Impact: Catches layout issues on all device sizes

Decision 5: Accessibility as First-Class Citizen
  - Context: Health app must be accessible to all users
  - Rationale: Tab navigation, screen readers, ARIA landmarks are critical
  - Evidence: Existing checkin.spec.ts has dedicated accessibility tests
  - Trade-offs: Additional test complexity, but non-negotiable for healthcare app
  - Impact: Ensures keyboard-only users and screen reader users can use dashboard

Decision 6: data-testid Selector Strategy
  - Context: Need stable, semantic selectors for test reliability
  - Rationale: Project standard uses data-testid attributes
  - Evidence: All existing tests use data-testid selectors
  - Trade-offs: Requires adding data-testid to components, but prevents brittle tests
  - Impact: Tests won't break when CSS classes change

Decision 7: Wait Strategies
  - Context: Dashboard loads data asynchronously from IndexedDB
  - Rationale: Use page.waitForTimeout(2000) after navigation for data loading
  - Evidence: Existing tests use similar wait strategies
  - Trade-offs: Fixed timeouts can be flaky in slow environments, but IndexedDB is fast
  - Impact: Tests are reliable in CI/CD environments

Decision 8: Comprehensive JSDoc Documentation
  - Context: Tests are complex with many scenarios
  - Rationale: Clear documentation helps future developers understand test intent
  - Evidence: Existing tests have detailed JSDoc headers
  - Trade-offs: More upfront writing, but saves time during maintenance
  - Impact: Tests are self-documenting and easy to debug

